  "Novelist, essayist and jazz critic; theorist of the blues as the basis of American culture and author of the four Scooter novels.",
  "Albert Lee Murray was born in Nokomis, Alabama, on 12 May 1916 and was adopted and raised in Magazine Point, on the outskirts of Mobile. He graduated from Tuskegee Institute in 1939, taught there, and served in the Army Air Forces during the Second World War. He took an MA at New York University in 1948 and studied at the Sorbonne in 1950, then returned to active duty in 1951 and retired from the US Air Force as a major in 1962. He settled in Harlem and published essays in magazines before collecting them as The Omni-Americans (1970), which was followed by a travel memoir, critical lectures, four novels about a boy called Scooter and several books on jazz, including the as-told-to autobiography of Count Basie. He taught at Barnard College in 1981-83 and held visiting posts elsewhere; his ideas influenced the critic Stanley Crouch and the trumpeter Wynton Marsalis, with whom he co-founded the programme that became Jazz at Lincoln Center in the 1980s. The National Book Critics Circle gave him its lifetime achievement award in 1996. He died in Harlem on 18 August 2013, aged ninety-seven.",
  "Murray's essays, fiction and jazz writing argue that American culture is fundamentally a blend of Black and white traditions and that the blues and jazz provide a model of heroic improvisation in the face of adversity.",
