  "The blues as an art of confronting adversity; the hybrid, 'Omni-American' character of national culture; heroism and improvisation; critique of social-science and protest images of Black life; Southern memory and education.",
  "Murray was a central voice in the long argument against protest fiction and Black separatism, and his writing on the blues and jazz shaped the institutional revival of jazz in the United States from the 1980s onwards.",
  bib_status="substantially complete",
  bib_note="Bibliography status, 1946-2000: substantially complete. Recorded as works: The Omni-Americans (1970), South to a Very Old Place (1971), The Hero and the Blues (1973), Train Whistle Guitar (1974), Stomping the Blues (1976), Good Morning Blues: The Autobiography of Count Basie (1985, as-told-to collaborator), The Spyglass Tree (1991), The Seven League Boots (1996), The Blue Devils of Nada (1996) and Trading Twelves (2000, co-edited correspondence with Ralph Ellison, recorded under Ellison with Murray as co-author). Unresolved: Reflections on Logic, Politics, and Reality: A Challenge to the Sacred Consensus of Contemporary American Thinking (Braimanna, 1989) appears in the Gale Contemporary Literary Criticism bibliography and in the Alabama Authors database, but its attribution to this Albert Murray has not been independently confirmed, so it has no work record. Not individually recorded: uncollected magazine essays and reviews. Outside the period: Conjugations and Reiterations (2001), From the Briarpatch File (2001), Murray Talks Music (2016) and the Library of America collected editions.",
